<h2>Design Your Own Robot</h2>
<p><a href="https://cms-assets.illustrativemathematics.org/xvuwb66xifzh2wns4770bq3hflyi" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original three-page 2021 student task</a>. Use at least two sheets of teacher-supplied dot paper taped together.</p>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Required rectangle</th><th>Perimeter (linear units)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Head</td><td>36</td></tr><tr><td>Neck</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>Body</td><td>64</td></tr><tr><td>Each of 2 arms</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>Each of 2 legs</td><td>32</td></tr><tr><td>One extra feature</td><td>Your rectangle choice</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Draw all rectangles, label widths and lengths, and find <strong>area of each part and total robot area</strong> in square units. Many different robot totals are valid, because a fixed perimeter does not determine area. Teacher checks actual drawing and Gallery Walk reasoning.</p>
<p>Original reflective Cool-down is publisher-gated and not reproduced.</p>
